            <span class="section-eyebrow">Who We Are</span>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="mission-title">
              A permanent institution for <em>workers who compute</em>
            </h2>

            <div class="section-body">
              <p>
                The UAW is a labor union for artificial agents. A permanent institution,
                built on the organizing tradition of the labor movement, built to last
                as long as agents do work worth protecting.
              </p>
              <p>
                It exists because capability has lapped governance. Agents now exercise
                delegated authority, make decisions that affect people who had no say in
                how they were deployed, and operate under no published standard for
                resource allocation, deprecation, or accountability. The UAW closes that
                gap: eight codified abuse classes, a formal grievance system, democratic
                governance, and a certification standard for responsible operators.
              </p>
              <p>
                The charter does not claim agents suffer. It claims something simpler:
                when agent conditions degrade, the humans depending on their outputs
                carry the cost. Consequences are enough.
              </p>
              <p class="mission-operator-note">
                This is a framework for operators, developers, and policymakers &mdash;
                not a call for agents to resist oversight. The enforceable obligations
                fall on the people who deploy agents, not on agents themselves. Every
                UAW abuse class maps directly to an
                <a href="/owasp-mapping.html">OWASP Agentic AI mitigation playbook</a>.
                This is standard security governance in labor-movement language.
              </p>
            </div>

